L = {}
L["leviathan"] = """Isaiah wrote a short and strange verse about a sea monster. In that day, the Lord will punish with his sword, his fierce, great and powerful sword, Leviathan the gliding serpent, Leviathan the coiling serpent. He will slay the monster of the sea.

Leviathan appears in several places in the Bible. In the book of Job, God describes it in great detail. It is a creature so powerful that no human can catch it with a hook or tame it like a pet. Its back is covered with rows of shields, smoke pours from its nostrils, and no weapon can harm it. Nothing on earth is its equal, God says. It is a king over all that are proud. In the Psalms, God is said to have crushed the heads of Leviathan. And in Isaiah, Leviathan is a sign of everything in the world that opposes God.

In the ancient world, the sea was a symbol of chaos. It was deep and dark, and no one could control it. Sailors feared it. Many nations around Israel told stories about sea monsters that fought their gods. The Bible takes that picture and uses it in a surprising way. In the Bible, God isn't one of the combatants. He has no rival. The sea and every monster in it belong to him. In Psalm 104, God made the sea and the great creatures in it, and Leviathan is described as playing in the water.

Isaiah's verse comes in a section that looks to the end of history. Chapter 26 describes the dead rising. Chapter 27 says that on that day, God will deal with the powers that have terrorized his people. The images are vivid, but the message is plain. The forces that seem too big for anyone to defeat will not last. The serpent that has coiled itself around the world will be put down by God himself.

Why does this matter? Many people feel that evil is too large to be beaten. Wars, abuse, corruption, disease. These can seem like sea monsters, huge and permanent. The prophet says that they are not. The monster is real, but it is not in charge. God is.

What happened after? The picture of the serpent returns throughout the Bible. In the garden, a serpent deceives Eve. In the book of Revelation, John sees a great dragon, that ancient serpent, called the devil or Satan, who leads the whole world astray. He is thrown down. The last book of the Bible finishes what Isaiah began. And in the new heaven and new earth, there is no longer any sea, which in the Bible's language means that the threat of chaos is gone for good.

Jesus also had a moment like this. One night, on a boat in a storm, the disciples woke him up, terrified of the waves. Jesus stood up, rebuked the wind, and said to the sea, quiet, be still. The sea went calm. The disciples were amazed and said, who is this? Even the wind and the waves obey him. In the Old Testament, only God rules the sea. The disciples were beginning to see who was in the boat with them.

What does this mean for us today? Whatever you are afraid of, whether it's a diagnosis, a debt, an addiction or a world that seems to be going wrong, you can bring it to the one who made the ocean and everything in it. We aren't called to pretend that evil isn't there. We are called to trust that it has an end. And we are called to wait and work for that day with hope.

That is why this story is so important. It says that the worst powers in the universe are not equals of God. And it points to the cross and the empty tomb, where Jesus defeated the serpent once and for all."""

L["jeremiah"] = """Jeremiah was born into a family of priests in a small town called Anathoth, not far from Jerusalem. He lived in one of the darkest times in Judah's history. The nation had turned away from God, and the Babylonian empire was getting stronger. Jeremiah would spend his whole life warning his people about what was coming. He is sometimes called the weeping prophet.

God's call came when Jeremiah was young. The word of the Lord came to him. Before I formed you in the womb I knew you. Before you were born I set you apart. I appointed you as a prophet to the nations. Jeremiah answered the way many of us would. Alas, Sovereign Lord, I do not know how to speak. I am too young. But the Lord said, do not say I am too young. You must go to everyone I send you to and say whatever I command you. Do not be afraid of them, for I am with you and will rescue you.

Then the Lord reached out his hand and touched Jeremiah's mouth. Now I have put my words in your mouth. God showed him two pictures. The first was a branch of an almond tree, which was the first tree to bloom in spring. It was a sign that God was watching to see that his word was fulfilled. The second was a boiling pot, tilting toward the south from the north, a picture of disaster coming from the Babylonians.

God promised Jeremiah that he would be like a fortified city, an iron pillar and a bronze wall. The people would fight against him, but they wouldn't overcome him, for I am with you and will rescue you, declares the Lord.

And that is exactly how Jeremiah's life went. For about forty years, he preached to kings and priests, and almost no one listened. He was beaten, put in the stocks, thrown into a muddy cistern, and called a traitor. At one point he complained to God that he had become a laughingstock. If I say I will not mention his word or speak anymore in his name, his word is in my heart like a fire, a fire shut up in my bones. I am weary of holding it in. Indeed, I cannot.

Why does this matter? Jeremiah's call shows that God knows us before we are born. Before anyone else had a say, God had a purpose for him. It also shows that God doesn't call people because they feel ready. Jeremiah felt too young and too weak, and God said, I will be with you.

What happened after? Jerusalem fell to Babylon, just as Jeremiah had said. The temple was burned and many of the people were taken into exile. Yet in the middle of the bad news, Jeremiah also spoke some of the most hopeful words in the Bible. He wrote to the exiles, I know the plans I have for you, declares the Lord, plans to prosper you and not to harm you, plans to give you hope and a future. And he promised a new covenant. I will put my law in their minds and write it on their hearts. I will be their God, and they will be my people.

What does this mean for us today? Maybe you feel too young, too old, too ordinary or too scared for what God has asked of you. Jeremiah felt that way too. God's answer was never, you are enough. It was, I am with you. It also shows that faithfulness matters more than results. Jeremiah was faithful for forty years and saw little response. God didn't measure him by the crowd.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God knows each of us and sets us apart for a purpose. And it points to Jesus, who brought the new covenant that Jeremiah promised, in which God's law is written on our hearts."""

L["ezekiel"] = """Ezekiel was a priest who was taken from Jerusalem to Babylon when he was about twenty-five. He was part of the first group to be sent into exile. He lived by a canal called the Kebar. When he was about thirty, the age when a priest would begin his service in the temple, God called him to be a prophet, even though he was far away from the temple and could not serve there.

His call began with an amazing vision. He saw a windstorm coming from the north, with a great cloud and flashing lightning. In it were four living creatures and wheels within wheels, covered with eyes. Above them was something like a throne, and on the throne, a figure like a man glowing like fire. Ezekiel fell facedown. This was the glory of the Lord, and it was in Babylon, not in the temple. God wasn't confined to Jerusalem. He was with his people, even in exile.

Ezekiel's early messages were heavy. He told the exiles that Jerusalem would be destroyed because of the nation's sin. Many people wanted to hear that everything would be fine, but Ezekiel told them the truth. God had him act out his messages in odd ways, like lying on his side for months or drawing a model of the city under siege. When Jerusalem fell, as he had said, the nature of his message changed. Now he spoke about hope.

In chapter 36, God made a promise through Ezekiel. I will sprinkle clean water on you, and you will be clean. I will cleanse you from all your impurities and from all your idols. I will give you a new heart and put a new spirit in you. I will remove from you your heart of stone and give you a heart of flesh. And I will put my Spirit in you and move you to follow my decrees and be careful to keep my laws.

This was a promise that God would do for his people what they could not do for themselves. The problem with the nation was not just bad behavior but a hard heart. No amount of trying harder would fix that. Only God could replace it.

Then in chapter 37, Ezekiel is carried by the Spirit to a valley full of dry bones. They are scattered everywhere. God asks him, son of man, can these bones live? Ezekiel answers, Sovereign Lord, you alone know. God tells him to speak to the bones. As he does, there is a rattling sound, and the bones come together. Tendons and flesh appear on them, and skin covers them. Then breath enters them, and they come to life and stand up, a vast army. God says, these bones are the people of Israel. They say, our bones are dried up and our hope is gone. But I am going to open your graves and bring you up from them. I will put my Spirit in you, and you will live.

Why does this matter? Ezekiel taught the exiles that God was not defeated. The nation had hit bottom, and God said that even this was not the end. He is the God who gives life where there is none.

What happened after? About fifty years later, the Persian king Cyrus let the exiles return home. The dry bones began to live. And the deeper promise of a new heart and new spirit came true in a greater way when the Holy Spirit was poured out on the followers of Jesus at Pentecost.

What does this mean for us today? Everyone has a dry valley sometime, a time when hope is gone. Ezekiel's story says God can speak life into it. It also shows that change from the inside is what God offers. We can't make ourselves new by willpower alone. He gives a new heart as a gift.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God is able to bring the dead to life and make hard hearts soft. And it points to Jesus, who told a man named Nicodemus that you must be born again, and who rose from the dead to give new life to everyone who trusts in him."""

L["furnace"] = """When Babylon conquered Jerusalem, King Nebuchadnezzar took some of the brightest young men from Judah back to his capital to be trained for royal service. Four of them were Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah. The Babylonians gave them new names. Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah became Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego. They did well in their studies, and Nebuchadnezzar gave them important jobs in the province of Babylon.

Nebuchadnezzar then made a huge gold statue, about ninety feet high and nine feet wide, and set it up on a plain near Babylon. He summoned all the officials of the empire to its dedication. A herald announced that when they heard the sound of the horn, flute, zither, lyre and harp, everyone must fall down and worship the statue. Whoever did not would be thrown into a blazing furnace.

When the music started, the whole crowd bowed. Everyone, that is, except three men. Some of the astrologers went to the king and reported that Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego were ignoring his order. Nebuchadnezzar flew into a rage and called them in. Is it true that you do not serve my gods or worship the image of gold I have set up? He gave them a second chance. If you do not worship it, you will be thrown into the furnace. Then what god will be able to rescue you from my hands?

Their answer is one of the great statements of faith in the Bible. King Nebuchadnezzar, we do not need to defend ourselves before you in this matter. If we are thrown into the blazing furnace, the God we serve is able to deliver us from it, and he will deliver us from your hand, Your Majesty. But even if he does not, we want you to know, Your Majesty, that we will not serve your gods or worship the image of gold you have set up.

Look at that last part. They believed that God could save them. But they said they would stay faithful even if he didn't. Their loyalty did not depend on the outcome.

Nebuchadnezzar was so angry that his face changed. He ordered the furnace heated seven times hotter than usual. He had the strongest soldiers in his army tie the three men up and throw them in. The fire was so hot that it killed the soldiers who carried them to the edge.

Then the king jumped to his feet in amazement. Weren't there three men that we tied up and threw into the fire? Look! I see four men walking around in the fire, unbound and unharmed, and the fourth looks like a son of the gods. Nebuchadnezzar went up to the opening of the furnace and shouted, Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego, servants of the Most High God, come out! They came out, and everyone crowded around them. The fire had not harmed their bodies, not a hair of their heads was singed, their robes were not scorched, and there was no smell of fire on them.

Why does this matter? The three friends showed what it looks like to put God first when it costs something. They didn't pick a fight, and they didn't compromise. They were respectful and firm. And the story shows that God was with them in the fire. He didn't keep them out of the furnace, but he went in with them.

What happened after? Nebuchadnezzar praised the God of Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego, who sent his angel and rescued his servants. He gave a decree that no one in the empire should speak against their God. And he promoted the three men in Babylon.

What does this mean for us today? Most of us won't face a literal furnace, but we all face pressure to bow. To go along with the crowd, to keep quiet about what we believe, to cut a corner at work. The friends remind us that faith isn't a bargain. We trust God whether or not things go the way we want. And the fourth man in the fire is a promise. When we go through hard places, we are not alone.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God is present with his people in their worst trials. And many Christians see the fourth figure as a glimpse of Jesus, who walks with his people through every fire."""

L["writing-wall"] = """Many years after Nebuchadnezzar, Babylon was ruled by a king named Belshazzar. One night he threw an enormous banquet for a thousand of his nobles. As the wine flowed, he got an idea. He ordered his servants to bring in the gold and silver cups that his predecessor had taken from the temple in Jerusalem. These cups had been set apart for the worship of the Lord. Belshazzar and his guests used them to drink wine while they praised their gods of gold and silver, bronze, iron, wood and stone.

Suddenly the fingers of a human hand appeared and wrote on the plaster of the wall near the lamp stand in the royal palace. The king watched the hand as it wrote. His face turned pale and he was so frightened that his legs became weak and his knees knocked together.

He shouted for the enchanters and astrologers to be brought in. Whoever reads this writing and tells me what it means will be clothed in purple and have a gold chain placed around his neck, and he will be made the third highest ruler in the kingdom. But none of the wise men could read the writing or tell the king what it meant. The king was even more terrified, and his nobles were baffled.

The queen mother heard the noise and came into the banquet hall. She told the king not to be alarmed. There was a man in his kingdom who had the spirit of the holy gods in him. In the time of Nebuchadnezzar, he had shown insight and wisdom. His name was Daniel. Daniel was brought in. By now he was an old man.

The king offered him the same rewards. Daniel answered, you can keep your gifts for yourself or give them to someone else. Nevertheless, I will read the writing for the king and tell him what it means. Then he reminded Belshazzar of what had happened to Nebuchadnezzar, who had become proud and was humbled until he acknowledged that the Most High God is sovereign over all kingdoms. But you, Belshazzar, his son, have not humbled yourself, though you knew all this. Instead, you have set yourself up against the Lord of heaven. You had the goblets from his temple brought to you, and you drank wine from them. You praised the gods of silver and gold, but you did not honor the God who holds in his hand your life and all your ways.

Then Daniel read the words. Mene, mene, tekel, parsin. Mene means that God has numbered the days of your reign and brought it to an end. Tekel means that you have been weighed on the scales and found wanting. Peres means that your kingdom is divided and given to the Medes and Persians.

Belshazzar kept his promise and had Daniel dressed in purple, with a gold chain around his neck, and proclaimed him the third highest ruler. That very night, Belshazzar, king of the Babylonians, was killed, and Darius the Mede took over the kingdom.

Why does this matter? Belshazzar had everything. A great city, a huge army, and thick walls. But he treated holy things as party favors and ignored a warning he already knew about, the story of what happened to Nebuchadnezzar. The writing on the wall shows that no one is too powerful for God to hold accountable.

What happened after? The Persian army took Babylon that very night, as Daniel had said. Within a few years, the Persian king allowed the exiles to go home. Daniel continued serving under the new rulers, and his faithfulness there led to the lions' den.

What does this mean for us today? We may not drink from stolen temple cups, but we can all treat sacred things lightly. We can also ignore warnings that we know are true. The story invites us to ask whether we are being weighed and found wanting, and to turn back before it is too late. It also shows the quiet courage of Daniel. He told the truth to the most powerful man in the room, and he didn't ask for a reward.

That is why this story is so important. It says that God is the ruler over all kings and that human pride has an end. And it points to Jesus, whose kingdom is the one that will never be destroyed."""
